Fix \(e=(u,c,c')\), and attach signs \(\sigma_{c,1}=1\), \(\sigma_{c,0}=-1\), \(\sigma_{c',1}=-1\), and \(\sigma_{c',0}=1\).  Conditional on \(X\), the definitions of \(p\) and \(m\) give
\[
E_P\!\left[\frac{\mathbf 1_{a,s}}{\widetilde p_{a,s}(X)}
\{\Delta Y_u-\widetilde m_{a,s,u}(X)\}\mid X\right]
=\frac{p_{a,s}(X)}{\widetilde p_{a,s}(X)}
\{m_{a,s,u}(X)-\widetilde m_{a,s,u}(X)\}.
\]
Summing with the four signs and adding the candidate restriction level yields
\[
E_P[S_e(O;\widetilde\eta)\mid X]-g_e(X)
=\sum_{(a,s)}\sigma_{a,s}
\{\widetilde m_{a,s,u}(X)-m_{a,s,u}(X)\}
\frac{\widetilde p_{a,s}(X)-p_{a,s}(X)}{\widetilde p_{a,s}(X)}.
\tag{1}
\]
This is an exact algebraic identity.  Its directional derivative at \(\widetilde\eta=\eta\) is zero because every summand on the right of (1) is a product of the two nuisance errors.  If the candidate propensities are clipped below \(\varepsilon/2\), Cauchy--Schwarz and the finite number of coordinates give
\[
\|E_P[S^{\mathrm{per}}(O;\widetilde\eta)\mid X]-g(X)\|_{P_X,2}
\le C_\varepsilon
\|\widetilde p-p\|_{P_X,4}\|\widetilde m-m\|_{P_X,4}.
\tag{2}
\]
At the truth, Definition \(\ref{def:orthogonal-complete-normal-signal}\) gives \(S_e(O;\eta)=g_e(X)+Z_{e,P}^{\mathrm{per}}(O)\).  The observable equalities in Theorem \(\ref{thm:observable-equivalence}\) give \(g_e(X)=0\) almost surely for every row in \(\mathcal E_{\mathrm{per}}\).  Therefore \(S^{\mathrm{per}}(O;\eta)=Z_P^{\mathrm{per}}(O)\) almost surely, completing the proof.
